WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:06.040 --> 00:00:14.860
[Rhythmic clanging sound]

00:00:15.100 --> 00:00:39.740
[Music]

00:00:39.900 --> 00:00:49.436
[Narrator:] In the busy, overcrowded, and highly competitive world of today, much of the story of mankind is recorded in terms of statistics.

00:00:49.436 --> 00:00:55.569
Statistics measure everything about us, from our average height to our average income.

00:00:55.569 --> 00:01:02.735
They compute the number of steps we will take in the course of one year or the number of miles we will ride in buses and taxis.

00:01:02.736 --> 00:01:07.869
[ Music ]

00:01:07.869 --> 00:01:10.569
Much statistical data is inconsequential.

00:01:10.569 --> 00:01:16.569
Columns of numbers evaluating trivia, measuring the unimportant fringe factors of life.

00:01:16.569 --> 00:01:20.169
But other statistics are all too significant,

00:01:20.169 --> 00:01:25.469
focusing a harsh light of reality on the grim shadows that dog humanity's footsteps.

00:01:25.469 --> 00:01:32.669
For example, statistics tell us that every year over 25,000 Americans take their own lives.

00:01:32.669 --> 00:01:39.569
And it is statistical fact that one of the 10 major causes of death in the United States is suicide.

00:01:39.569 --> 00:01:46.535
The urge for self-destruction walks in step with thousands of those who make up our civilian population.

00:01:46.536 --> 00:01:51.702
[ Music ]

00:01:51.702 --> 00:01:58.235
And, as a natural consequence, it also marches alongside some of those who wear the uniform of the United States Army.

00:01:58.236 --> 00:02:05.069
The stories of five such individuals will now be told, and the endings will not be happy.

00:02:05.069 --> 00:02:10.102
But the point and purpose to be made here is that such tragedies are not inevitable.

00:02:10.102 --> 00:02:12.535
Prevention is possible.

00:02:12.536 --> 00:02:20.069
For, as you will see, the suicidal individual almost always provides clues that give advance warning of his intentions.

00:02:20.069 --> 00:02:23.935
Watch for these clues. Remember these clues.

00:02:23.936 --> 00:02:26.636
They could someday enable you to save a life.

00:02:26.636 --> 00:02:33.602
[ Music ]

00:02:33.602 --> 00:02:43.835
From his first day in uniform, Private Phillip G. Owens seemed totally unable to adjust to the new world in which he found himself.

00:02:43.836 --> 00:02:48.036
Even the relatively simple requirements of basic training were beyond him.

00:02:48.036 --> 00:02:52.536
He lagged behind his fellow soldiers, out of step in both mind and body.

00:02:52.536 --> 00:03:05.302
[ Music ]

00:03:05.302 --> 00:03:08.802
His failure to keep the pace quickly singled him out for attention.

00:03:08.802 --> 00:03:11.735
Many believed he just simply wasn't trying.

00:03:11.736 --> 00:03:23.769
[ Music ]

00:03:23.769 --> 00:03:27.702
Phil's awkwardness was not confined to military matters.

00:03:27.702 --> 00:03:42.935
[ Music ]

00:03:42.936 --> 00:03:45.169
What do you do with a misfit like Owens?

00:03:45.169 --> 00:03:50.335
Maybe the simplest thing is to ignore him, which is what his fellow soldiers did.

00:03:50.336 --> 00:03:56.369
They saw him as a loner who was always picking himself out a private corner, and who would make no effort to be friendly.

00:03:56.369 --> 00:04:06.135
[Owens sits alone on the bleachers after being shunned by the rest of the soldiers.]

00:04:06.136 --> 00:04:14.002
When Owens started a regular chain of visits to the dispensary, the men in his platoon nodded knowingly and said, "That figures."

00:04:14.002 --> 00:04:21.335
[Owens:] I don't know what it is, but I get these headaches and I don't know, I can't stand it.

00:04:21.336 --> 00:04:23.136
[Attendant:] Yeah, I know all about the headaches.

00:04:23.136 --> 00:04:27.836
You've been riding the sick book pretty good, Owens. Pretty good.

00:04:27.836 --> 00:04:30.336
[Owens:] I haven't been riding the sick book.

00:04:30.336 --> 00:04:34.669
Sometimes my head feels like it's gonna explode.

00:04:34.669 --> 00:04:41.002
I don't know what to do.

00:04:41.002 --> 00:04:44.968
[Attendant:] OK. OK. Go out in the hall and wait.

00:04:44.969 --> 00:04:49.169
The doctor will see you in a little while.

00:04:49.169 --> 00:04:52.102
[Narrator:] His fellow soldiers were an unsympathetic audience.

00:04:52.102 --> 00:04:55.935
[ Music ]

00:04:55.936 --> 00:05:05.036
They felt that if good, wholesome Army chow wasn't up to Owens' high standards, well, that was just too bad.

00:05:05.036 --> 00:05:11.936
And if he was too delicate to sleep on an Army cot, tough.

00:05:11.936 --> 00:05:16.502
Owens' apparent lack of interests, his seeming indifference to his surroundings,

00:05:16.502 --> 00:05:21.835
his apparent unwillingness to make any effort to get along, inevitably led to further difficulties.

00:05:21.836 --> 00:05:23.602
[Commander:] Good throwing position.

00:05:23.602 --> 00:05:25.168
Ready position.

00:05:25.169 --> 00:05:27.735
Put your finger in the pull ring.

00:05:27.736 --> 00:05:29.669
Look at your target down range.

00:05:29.669 --> 00:05:32.235
Pull pin!

00:05:32.236 --> 00:05:37.369
Throw! Throw the grenade, trainee!

00:05:37.369 --> 00:05:41.002
Trainee, get rid of that grenade!

00:05:41.002 --> 00:05:44.902
Trainee, get rid of the grenade! Hit the pulp!

00:05:44.902 --> 00:05:48.102
[ Explosion ]

00:05:48.102 --> 00:05:51.435
[Voice over radio:] The firing line is clear. On your feet.

00:05:51.436 --> 00:05:54.436
Pick up your container and move to your left and to the rear behind the wall.

00:05:54.436 --> 00:06:02.102
[Commander:] Get on your feet, trainee, and move out!

00:06:02.102 --> 00:06:07.535
Come on, trainee. Move out!

00:06:07.536 --> 00:06:11.202
[Officer:] Owens, get over here!

00:06:11.202 --> 00:06:13.835
I watched you from the tower. Can't you do anything right?

00:06:13.836 --> 00:06:25.102
I'm turning you in.

00:06:25.102 --> 00:06:27.268
[Man at desk:] What's the trouble, Owens?

00:06:27.269 --> 00:06:32.069
[Owens:] I don't know, sir. I try to do things right, but nothing seems to work.

00:06:32.069 --> 00:06:33.935
I don't know.

00:06:33.936 --> 00:06:36.969
[Officer:] He's not trying. Take a look at him, sir.

00:06:36.969 --> 00:06:39.635
I've spoken to him time and again about his appearance.

00:06:39.636 --> 00:06:42.402
He doesn't shave or take care of his clothes or anything.

00:06:42.402 --> 00:06:46.568
[Owens:] I do try. These headaches.

00:06:46.569 --> 00:06:48.802
[Officer:] He's just trying to work a deal, sir.

00:06:48.802 --> 00:06:51.068
I know about these headaches.

00:06:51.069 --> 00:06:55.902
[Officer at desk:] You've been on sick call several times and they haven't found anything wrong with you.

00:06:55.902 --> 00:06:57.635
[Owens:] I know, sir, but...

00:06:57.636 --> 00:07:02.102
[Officer at desk:] You've just got to pull 
yourself together and that's all there is to it.

00:07:02.102 --> 00:07:07.168
[Owens:] Sir, I don't want to make any trouble.

00:07:07.169 --> 00:07:10.469
Sometimes I feel so bad.

00:07:10.469 --> 00:07:14.202
I think I'm going to kill myself. I don't know what to do!

00:07:14.202 --> 00:07:20.102
[Officer at desk:] Now that's stupid talk! Pull yourself together and act like a man!

00:07:20.102 --> 00:07:25.402
[Owens:] Yes, sir.

00:07:25.402 --> 00:07:30.335
[ Music ]

00:07:30.336 --> 00:07:33.702
[Narrator:] Something new has been added to Owens' torrent of troubles.

00:07:33.702 --> 00:07:36.035
A suggestion of suicide.

00:07:36.036 --> 00:07:39.069
It has been labeled as stupid talk, and perhaps it is.

00:07:39.069 --> 00:07:42.769
For isn't it true that if they talk about it, they never do it?

00:07:42.769 --> 00:07:52.502
[ Music ]

00:07:52.502 --> 00:07:54.268
[Solider 1:] Sandy Koufax!

00:07:54.269 --> 00:07:57.735
Sandy Koufax Owens, the man with the golden arm!

00:07:57.736 --> 00:08:07.636
[Soldier 2:] Oh, boy. Well, time for lights out now.

00:08:07.636 --> 00:08:16.302
[Soldier 1:] Got to get your rest now, champ. Let's go beddy-bye.

00:08:16.302 --> 00:08:18.635
[Narrator:] Portrait of a man deep in depression.

00:08:18.636 --> 00:08:24.336
Restless, apathetic, neglectful of appearance, indifferent to environment.

00:08:24.336 --> 00:08:30.169
A man alone in a crowd, isolated and maladjusted.

00:08:30.169 --> 00:08:34.469
Opening his mouth only to complain of headaches, to confess his own helplessness,

00:08:34.469 --> 00:08:39.369
and, oh yes, to announce that he is contemplating self-destruction.

00:08:39.369 --> 00:08:46.435
Does he really need help, or would a swift kick straighten him out?

00:08:46.436 --> 00:08:52.436
It's a little late for conjecture, for Phil Owens has found a solution to his problems.

00:08:52.436 --> 00:08:59.369
A solution that waits patiently under a fold in his blanket.

00:08:59.369 --> 00:09:32.902
[ Music ]

00:09:32.902 --> 00:09:39.268
The CQ should not be surprised to discover that Owens is not in his sack,

00:09:39.269 --> 00:09:43.202
for Owens' desperation has been announced in many ways.

00:09:43.202 --> 00:09:45.102
His inability to adjust.

00:09:45.102 --> 00:09:47.035
His drift into isolation.

00:09:47.036 --> 00:09:50.336
His loss of appetite, his insomnia.

00:09:50.336 --> 00:09:52.202
His growing depression.

00:09:52.202 --> 00:09:54.335
They were all clear signs.

00:09:54.336 --> 00:09:59.236
They were all clues and an experienced eye could have seen that they added up

00:09:59.236 --> 00:10:13.769
to something more than absence without official leave, that they predicted an act of desperate finality.

00:10:13.769 --> 00:10:15.069
[Commander:] Oh, no!

00:10:15.069 --> 00:10:24.735
[ Music ]

00:10:24.736 --> 00:10:29.436
[Commander:] Call the hospital and [?] to get over here.

00:10:29.436 --> 00:10:38.669
[The soldiers scramble to see what happened to Owens.]

00:10:38.669 --> 00:10:41.602
[Commander:] Dillon, get in here and give me a hand!

00:10:41.602 --> 00:10:52.135
[ Music ]

00:10:52.136 --> 00:10:56.769
[Narrator:] No, it is not true that if they talk about it they never do it.

00:10:56.769 --> 00:11:00.669
A suicidal threat, particularly when accompanied by insomnia,

00:11:00.669 --> 00:11:11.769
loss of appetite, complaints of illness, and other indications of deep depression should never be ignored.

00:11:11.769 --> 00:11:16.235
Picture of a confident, capable Army instructor.

00:11:16.236 --> 00:11:22.102
His name is Chris Warren and the external image he presents to the world is one that many could envy.

00:11:22.102 --> 00:11:27.368
But the brightly polished exterior is only a lid held tight on a simmering cauldron.

00:11:27.369 --> 00:11:34.802
Though he doesn't show it, Chris Warren is a man under considerable pressure.

00:11:34.802 --> 00:11:37.735
Problem number one: money.

00:11:37.736 --> 00:11:44.236
He spends too much of it and then saddles himself with high-interest loans that take huge bites out of his paycheck.

00:11:44.236 --> 00:11:49.969
[ Music ]

00:11:49.969 --> 00:11:54.002
Problem number two: an irresponsible, immature wife who lacks the discipline

00:11:54.002 --> 00:11:59.102
and restraint that could help Chris to work his way out of his difficulty.

00:11:59.102 --> 00:12:01.035
[Lil:] I wish you didn't have to go to work tonight.

00:12:01.036 --> 00:12:04.069
I'm tired of being alone all the time.

00:12:04.069 --> 00:12:07.602
[Chris:] I wasn't crazy about getting a second job either, Lil,

00:12:07.602 --> 00:12:11.402
but I've got to get the money to pay these bills someplace.

00:12:11.402 --> 00:12:13.068
[Lil:] Don't blame me!

00:12:13.069 --> 00:12:16.469
It's not my fault. I'm doing the best I can.

00:12:16.469 --> 00:12:19.069
[Chris:] All right, Lil. Nobody's blaming you.

00:12:19.069 --> 00:12:22.602
But I know what I've got to do.

00:12:22.602 --> 00:12:27.735
[Lil:] Well, I know what I'm going to do.

00:12:27.736 --> 00:12:30.536
[Chris:] What's that, honey?

00:12:30.536 --> 00:12:32.902
[Lil:] Never mind.

00:12:32.902 --> 00:12:39.068
[Narrator:] Chris reacts to her threat with smiling good nature and then follows up with a hurt puppy dog expression.

00:12:39.069 --> 00:12:49.835
But each attitude only increases her irritation and contempt and strengthens her determination.

00:12:49.836 --> 00:12:54.336
Problem number three: a very serious illness which strikes his daughter.

00:12:54.336 --> 00:12:59.969
Still, for her sake, he maintains the image, the smile, the confidence.

00:12:59.969 --> 00:13:05.169
[ Music ]

00:13:05.169 --> 00:13:09.369
But then comes a blow that catches him right on the button.

00:13:09.369 --> 00:13:15.402
Verification for his own disillusioned eyes of the fact that his wife is running around with other men.

00:13:15.402 --> 00:13:19.935
Men with fat wallets and trim automobiles.

00:13:19.936 --> 00:13:26.836
[ Music ]

00:13:26.836 --> 00:13:30.936
The pressure now has increased to a point beyond containment.

00:13:30.936 --> 00:13:34.369
Cracks begin to show.

00:13:34.369 --> 00:13:38.435
The image of a confident, cheerful soldier starts to come apart at the seams.

00:13:38.436 --> 00:13:40.269
Sudden excessive drinking.

00:13:40.269 --> 00:13:42.235
Sudden neglect of appearance.

00:13:42.236 --> 00:13:44.169
Sudden despondency.

00:13:44.169 --> 00:13:45.902
These are the first clues.

00:13:45.902 --> 00:13:49.968
His friends are aware of the dramatic change, but avoid involvement,

00:13:49.969 --> 00:13:56.669
thinking perhaps that he'll get back to normal with a little time.

00:13:56.669 --> 00:14:01.935
But Chris Warren is headed down, not up and is gaining momentum rapidly.

00:14:01.936 --> 00:14:04.769
He is now careless and neglectful of his work.

00:14:04.769 --> 00:14:13.635
Sinking into a deep pool of self-pity, he begins to feel that the world and his superiors are all against him.

00:14:13.636 --> 00:14:17.869
[Captain:] You were late for class this morning, Warren. That's the second time this week.

00:14:17.869 --> 00:14:26.635
[Chris:] I know. I ran into a little traffic and I...I'm sorry.

00:14:26.636 --> 00:14:31.769
[Captain:] Yeah, I know you're sorry, but if you don't snap out if it, you're going to find yourself in big trouble.

00:14:31.769 --> 00:14:34.869
[Chris:] I do my work. You don't have to lean on me.

00:14:34.869 --> 00:14:39.369
[Captain:] No you don't! Your work's been sloppy and you're on the phone with your wife all day,

00:14:39.369 --> 00:14:41.735
and you walk around here looking like an unmade bed.

00:14:41.736 --> 00:14:51.336
Look at yourself!

00:14:51.336 --> 00:14:56.669
[Chris:] I've had some pretty bad personal problems lately and I'm,

00:14:56.669 --> 00:15:00.335
I guess I ought to talk to somebody about them, I...

00:15:00.336 --> 00:15:04.769
maybe I'll see the chaplain.

00:15:04.769 --> 00:15:07.002
[Captain:] You see anybody you like, but I'm telling you this.

00:15:07.002 --> 00:15:09.868
If you don't shape up in a hurry, I'm turning you in to the old man!

00:15:09.869 --> 00:15:12.735
Now you remember that!

00:15:12.736 --> 00:15:17.802
[Narrator:] As his problems mount, Chris Warren becomes correspondingly less able to cope with them.

00:15:17.802 --> 00:15:25.435
The internal pressure continues to build higher and higher.

00:15:25.436 --> 00:15:33.902
Alcohol temporarily minimizes the significance of his problems, but it also moves him closer to disaster.

00:15:33.902 --> 00:15:36.235
[Chris:] Women.

00:15:36.236 --> 00:15:37.969
Do it every time.

00:15:37.969 --> 00:15:41.369
Well, one for the road, huh?

00:15:41.369 --> 00:15:47.269
I gotta get back to the post.

00:15:47.269 --> 00:15:49.669
[Bartender:] That'll be two bucks, sarge.

00:15:49.669 --> 00:15:57.302
[ Music ]

00:15:57.302 --> 00:16:01.368
[Chris:] Keep it.

00:16:01.369 --> 00:16:05.002
[Narrator:] And now he makes his most serious mistake.

00:16:05.002 --> 00:16:13.535
[ Music ]

00:16:13.536 --> 00:16:35.536
[Bartender:] Hey, sarge!

00:16:35.536 --> 00:16:38.636
Military police, please.

00:16:38.636 --> 00:16:43.469
[Chris:] Sir, it was unintentional.

00:16:43.469 --> 00:16:49.335
My wife's been running around with another man.

00:16:49.336 --> 00:16:52.802
[General:] I'm sorry, Warren. The case is out of my hands.

00:16:52.802 --> 00:16:55.302
[Chris:] Sir, my record's been good up until now.

00:16:55.302 --> 00:17:01.135
[General:] Not recently. You used to be one our best non-comms, but the reports I've gotten from the sergeant here

00:17:01.136 --> 00:17:03.602
and from others lately have not been good.

00:17:03.602 --> 00:17:08.535
[Chris:] Sir, I've got trouble.

00:17:08.536 --> 00:17:16.502
I can't pay my bills. I can't take any more trouble.

00:17:16.502 --> 00:17:19.302
[General:] Your offense is currently under investigation, Warren.

00:17:19.302 --> 00:17:23.535
The outcome of that investigation will determine how this matter is settled.

00:17:23.536 --> 00:17:25.402
That's all, Warren.

00:17:25.402 --> 00:17:28.202
Sergeant, take this man back to his quarters.

00:17:28.202 --> 00:17:31.602
Dismissed.

00:17:31.602 --> 00:17:35.435
[Narrator:] Chris Warren is now reduced to the silence of desperation.

00:17:35.436 --> 00:17:45.536
A desperation that flashed many unseen danger signals and shouted many unheard cries for help.

00:17:45.536 --> 00:17:49.236
Portrait of a man whose normally bright and confident exterior

00:17:49.236 --> 00:17:53.169
belied the fact that he too could be overwhelmed by troubles.

00:17:53.169 --> 00:17:55.035
But it was all there.

00:17:55.036 --> 00:17:59.836
The extreme change of behavior patterns, the drinking, the loss of efficiency.

00:17:59.836 --> 00:18:03.469
The inability to cope with family and professional problems.

00:18:03.469 --> 00:18:05.335
The physical deterioration.

00:18:05.336 --> 00:18:08.536
All clues predicting disaster.

00:18:08.536 --> 00:18:29.002
[ Music ]

00:18:29.002 --> 00:18:34.435
Never assume that an individual exhibiting the clues shown by Chris Warren will just suddenly snap out of it.

00:18:34.436 --> 00:18:41.736
By directing him to professional help, you may avoid a tragedy.

00:18:41.736 --> 00:18:46.036
This young lady is Laura Benson, Specialist-4, Women's Army Corps.

00:18:46.036 --> 00:18:53.236
Laura is the new x-ray technician in the post hospital.

00:18:53.236 --> 00:18:56.402
[Laura:] There. That wasn't painful, was it?

00:18:56.402 --> 00:18:59.768
[Patient:] No.

00:18:59.769 --> 00:19:03.802
[Laura:] OK. I guess that's all we can do right now.

00:19:03.802 --> 00:19:07.102
The results of your x-ray will be sent to your doctor.

00:19:07.102 --> 00:19:11.835
[Patient:] Thank you.

00:19:11.836 --> 00:19:17.702
[Narrator:] Since Laura is pleasant, pretty, and friendly, personal overtures from the male population are inevitable.

00:19:17.702 --> 00:19:25.702
In fact, she sometimes seems to invite them.
But when they come, her reaction is startling.

00:19:25.702 --> 00:19:26.602
[Bill:] Oh, Laura?

00:19:26.602 --> 00:19:27.535
[Laura:] Yes, Bill, what is it?

00:19:27.536 --> 00:19:29.969
[Bill:] Could you take over for me for an hour or so?

00:19:29.969 --> 00:19:32.002
Phillips has to go back to his company for training.

00:19:32.002 --> 00:19:33.468
[Laura:] Oh, sure, Bill.

00:19:33.469 --> 00:19:36.669
Anything you say. Anything at all.

00:19:36.669 --> 00:19:38.169
[Bill:] Anything?

00:19:38.169 --> 00:19:40.402
[Laura:] Well, you just say it!

00:19:40.402 --> 00:19:43.035
[Bill:] Well, how about a date Saturday night?

00:19:43.036 --> 00:19:52.369
[Laura:] Oh, I'm afraid not. My cousin is coming in from Philadelphia and I'll be tied up most of the weekend.

00:19:52.369 --> 00:19:55.602
[Bill:] You have more cousins than I have ever seen.

00:19:55.602 --> 00:20:00.168
[Laura:] Stop it! Stop it! Leave me alone!

00:20:00.169 --> 00:20:03.169
[Bill:] OK, OK. Anything you say.

00:20:03.169 --> 00:20:07.702
Just don't get hysterical.

00:20:07.702 --> 00:20:11.002
[Laura:] Just keep your hands to yourself!

00:20:11.002 --> 00:20:17.735
I know what you want. I know.

00:20:17.736 --> 00:20:22.436
[Narrator:] Laura's relationships with all her friends and coworkers are surface affairs only.

00:20:22.436 --> 00:20:26.602
Any attempt at closer contact brings a violent overreaction.

00:20:26.602 --> 00:20:33.002
Even an innocuous gab session at a nearby table triggers her sensitivity.

00:20:33.002 --> 00:20:35.135
[Laura:] Are they laughing at us?

00:20:35.136 --> 00:20:40.236
[Laura's Friend:] No, of course not.

00:20:40.236 --> 00:20:45.202
[Laura:] I wish they'd stop bothering me.

00:20:45.202 --> 00:20:49.102
[Laura's friend:] Laura, you get yourself all upset about nothing. Why don't you take it easy?

00:20:49.102 --> 00:20:55.002
They're probably laughing at some joke they just made up.

00:20:55.002 --> 00:20:59.802
[Narrator:] Then she meets Roy.

00:20:59.802 --> 00:21:05.802
Mild-mannered, passive, and undemanding, Roy seems made to order for her needs.

00:21:05.802 --> 00:21:11.435
Laura has found her ideal man and she holds tight.

00:21:11.436 --> 00:21:23.436
But the snug security she derives from a union in which she is easily the dominant force is not to continue for long.

00:21:23.436 --> 00:21:25.769
[Laura:] When will you be leaving?

00:21:25.769 --> 00:21:28.935
[Roy:] On the 22nd of August.

00:21:28.936 --> 00:21:31.936
[Laura:] Do you know how long you'll be over there?

00:21:31.936 --> 00:21:35.369
[Roy:] I don't know, Laura. You know how those things are.

00:21:35.369 --> 00:21:39.702
[Laura:] I suppose you'll be like the rest of the fellas.

00:21:39.702 --> 00:21:42.568
You'll forget all about me as soon as you get overseas.

00:21:42.569 --> 00:21:46.935
[Roy:] No, I won't forget about you, Laura.

00:21:46.936 --> 00:21:49.902
[Laura:] You won't write to me. I know.

00:21:49.902 --> 00:21:54.235
[Roy:] I'll write. Don't worry about it. I will write.

00:21:54.236 --> 00:21:57.136
[Laura:] Roy, are you sure?

00:21:57.136 --> 00:22:03.169
[Roy:] Of course, honey.

00:22:03.169 --> 00:22:09.335
[Narrator:] Laura Benson, who is hypersensitive to imagined insults and overreacts hysterically to innocuous incident

00:22:09.336 --> 00:22:15.369
will not be able to accept Roy's departure lightly.

00:22:15.369 --> 00:22:18.269
[Captain:] Look, this is no good. There's movement and it's too dark.

00:22:18.269 --> 00:22:21.469
[Laura:] Captain, I'm terribly sorry, but I've just had so much work.

00:22:21.469 --> 00:22:25.402
[Captain:] Look, Miss Benson, you're just going to have to take this out and do it over again.

00:22:25.402 --> 00:22:28.802
[Laura:] Yes, Captain. I'll take care of it immediately.

00:22:28.802 --> 00:22:30.702
[Bill:] Laura!

00:22:30.702 --> 00:22:32.868
[Laura:] Yes, what is it?

00:22:32.869 --> 00:22:34.002
[Bill:] Now, wait a minute.

00:22:34.002 --> 00:22:37.168
Now, look, you're way behind on these referral slips.

00:22:37.169 --> 00:22:44.302
[Laura:] All right. All right. I'll have them ready for you as soon as I can.

00:22:44.302 --> 00:22:48.635
[Narrator:] Laura has already begun to wave unconscious distress signals.

00:22:48.636 --> 00:22:50.536
She's explosively irritable.

00:22:50.560 --> 00:22:52.969
Her job is suddenly too much for her.

00:22:52.969 --> 00:22:57.240
She feels that her coworkers are unfair and overcritical.

00:22:59.940 --> 00:23:04.800
And then comes the trio of clues that can signal deep, dreary depression.

00:23:04.800 --> 00:23:11.320
The significant combination of insomnia, loss of appetite, and physical complaints.

00:23:15.668 --> 00:23:18.900
[Doctor:] Thank you for helping out.  [Laura's friend:] You're welcome, sir.

00:23:31.902 --> 00:23:36.635
[Doctor:] Can you think of anything in particular that might have upset you?

00:23:36.636 --> 00:23:43.736
[Laura:] Well, I guess it all began when my boyfriend went overseas.

00:23:43.736 --> 00:23:47.869
He left and, well, he hasn't even written to me.

00:23:47.869 --> 00:23:49.769
[Doctor:] When did he leave?

00:23:49.769 --> 00:23:52.035
[Laura:] August 22nd.

00:23:52.036 --> 00:23:54.636
[Doctor:] August the 22nd, that's only two weeks.

00:23:54.636 --> 00:23:57.736
He's only been there a week.

00:23:57.736 --> 00:23:59.602
[Laura:] He hasn't written, doctor.

00:23:59.602 --> 00:24:02.702
And I really don't think he will.

00:24:02.702 --> 00:24:07.268
[Doctor:] You can hardly expect to receive a letter in that length of time.

00:24:07.269 --> 00:24:11.935
[Laura:] I know, doctor, but it's just that I really don't think he will write.

00:24:11.936 --> 00:24:14.936
And I've been so worried.

00:24:14.940 --> 00:24:21.100
I don't know what to do. I'm just so mixed-up inside.

00:24:25.140 --> 00:24:30.336
[Narrator:] She bombards Roy with a rapid-fire series of angry, possessive, accusing letters.

00:24:30.336 --> 00:24:35.536
Compelled by her own hysterical illogical evaluation of a personal crisis.

00:24:35.536 --> 00:24:40.669
[ Music ]

00:24:40.669 --> 00:24:48.635
But her overpowering letters trigger a polite response that, for her, is a disastrous rejection.

00:24:48.636 --> 00:24:53.836
[Roy:] And while I feel you're a wonderful girl, Laura, I think this thing has gotten too serious.

00:24:53.836 --> 00:25:00.436
Maybe you ought to go out with other fellas and learn to relax and enjoy yourself more.

00:25:00.436 --> 00:25:03.536
[Narrator:] Laura's world is toppling.

00:25:03.536 --> 00:25:08.169
And she must step out from under the weight of a problem with which she cannot cope.

00:25:08.169 --> 00:25:12.235
None have responded to the clues that were evident in her behavior,

00:25:12.236 --> 00:25:16.136
and without help or guidance, she can see only one way to turn.

00:25:16.136 --> 00:25:39.502
[ Music ]

00:25:39.502 --> 00:25:41.520
[Laura bangs her locker open to retrieve some pills.]

00:25:41.660 --> 00:26:19.680
[Music]

00:26:19.860 --> 00:26:27.960
Later, as she begins to feel herself drifting into unconsciousness, Laura Benson attempts one 
last outcry.

00:26:27.969 --> 00:26:30.902
One last conscious call for help.

00:26:30.902 --> 00:26:34.140
[Rotary phone dial sound]

00:26:46.540 --> 00:26:53.280
[Laura:] Mother! Mother, I can't take it any longer.

00:26:56.700 --> 00:27:00.960
No! No, you don't care about me!

00:27:01.460 --> 00:27:03.860
You've never cared about me!

00:27:03.860 --> 00:27:10.120
And now, now Roy doesn't care about me.

00:27:15.660 --> 00:27:20.280
I'm going to show all of you.

00:27:25.160 --> 00:27:31.420
[Phone dangles from its cord and knocks against the wall.]

00:27:31.436 --> 00:27:36.336
[Narrator:] Never minimize the significant trio of insomnia, loss of appetite, and physical complaints,

00:27:36.340 --> 00:27:39.800
for they can be an indication of deep depression,

00:27:39.800 --> 00:27:43.420
a human being in serious need of help.

00:27:47.200 --> 00:27:50.600
This soldier's name is Hal Whitney.

00:27:50.602 --> 00:27:53.868
He does not top the popularity polls.

00:27:53.869 --> 00:28:00.469
In fact, more than a few men in his outfit have toyed with the idea of pushing him off a roof, in front of a speeding truck,

00:28:00.469 --> 00:28:02.902
or maybe under an M60 tank.

00:28:02.902 --> 00:28:05.435
Hal's a wheeler-dealer, a manipulator.

00:28:05.436 --> 00:28:12.436
A loudmouth bragger who's always looking to con himself into something good or out of something bad.

00:28:12.436 --> 00:28:14.936
Always looking to clip the other guys.

00:28:14.936 --> 00:28:18.769
The trouble is, the other guys are smarter than Hal is.

00:28:18.769 --> 00:28:24.835
They get his number fast and lately, in his not-too-subtle attempts to take advantage of his buddies,

00:28:24.840 --> 00:28:28.960
he is striking out with depressing regularity.

00:28:30.640 --> 00:28:34.960
[Hal:] Hey, Chuck. How about doing me a favor, huh?

00:28:34.969 --> 00:28:36.935
[Chuck:] Yeah?

00:28:36.936 --> 00:28:39.836
[Hal:] How about loaning me a fat 12 just until Tuesday, OK?

00:28:39.836 --> 00:28:41.902
[Chuck:] Nothing doing.

00:28:41.902 --> 00:28:44.202
[Hal:] Oh, why not? Come on, just 'til Tuesday.

00:28:44.202 --> 00:28:47.035
What's the big deal? What am I asking you?
Twelve dollars 'til next Tuesday.

00:28:47.036 --> 00:28:48.936
I'd do the same for you.

00:28:48.936 --> 00:28:53.036
[Chuck:] I said nothing doing. Now, look, you've put the bite on me too many times already.

00:28:53.036 --> 00:28:54.702
I'm getting sick and tired of it.

00:28:54.702 --> 00:28:57.802
[Hal:] Me? Me? How can you say that, Chuck?

00:28:57.802 --> 00:28:59.902
I mean really, I'm surprised. I really am.

00:28:59.902 --> 00:29:02.468
[Chuck:] Well, don't be too surprised.

00:29:02.469 --> 00:29:05.235
Look, you're in with half the guys in the company already.

00:29:05.240 --> 00:29:08.100
Money, equipment, something...anything!

00:29:08.100 --> 00:29:10.740
[Hal:] Boy, you guys are small, you know that?

00:29:10.740 --> 00:29:13.460
The last outfit I was in, you could depend on a buddy to do you a favor.

00:29:13.860 --> 00:29:16.440
You guys wouldn't lift a finger if somebody was dying right in front of you!

00:29:18.200 --> 00:29:21.160
Man, I gotta get out of this outfit.

00:29:21.169 --> 00:29:30.169
[ Music ]

00:29:30.169 --> 00:29:41.935
[Narrator:] Hal Whitney, totally baffled by the world's unsympathetic response to the pressing problems of Hal Whitney.

00:29:41.936 --> 00:29:48.402
Convinced he is being taken advantage of, Hal counterattacks with all the fury he can muster.

00:29:48.402 --> 00:29:50.568
[Sergeant:] All right. We'll see you. Goodbye.

00:29:50.569 --> 00:29:52.502
[Hal:] Sergeant, why am I on that KP list again?

00:29:52.502 --> 00:29:54.502
[Sergeant:] Forget it, Whitney, you're on KP and you're going to pull the assignment.

00:29:54.502 --> 00:29:55.635
No funny stuff this time.

00:29:55.636 --> 00:29:56.802
[Hal:] Why me?

00:29:56.802 --> 00:29:59.168
Forty-three guys in the barracks and you're always picking on me.

00:29:59.169 --> 00:30:02.402
[Sergeant:] Sutton's in the hospital and you're the next name on the duty roster.

00:30:02.402 --> 00:30:04.002
Nobody's picking on you.

00:30:04.002 --> 00:30:05.736
[Hal:] Well, I'm not going to hold still for it.

00:30:05.740 --> 00:30:08.340
Look, I'm tired of being pushed around. I want to see the old man.

00:30:08.340 --> 00:30:10.420
[Sergeant:] Suit yourself.

00:30:12.240 --> 00:30:17.436
[Narrator:] His fellow soldiers grow increasingly contemptuous of Hal's aggressive but futile attempts

00:30:17.440 --> 00:30:21.280
to outwit both the Army and life in general.

00:30:24.402 --> 00:30:26.460
[Captain:] And it is his turn, Sergeant?[Sergeant:] Yes, sir, it is.

00:30:26.460 --> 00:30:28.240
[Captain:] That's all. [Sergeant:] Sir.

00:30:31.802 --> 00:30:34.236
[Hal:] I pull my share of assignments, sir.

00:30:34.236 --> 00:30:36.436
This isn't fair! He's sticking it to me.

00:30:36.436 --> 00:30:39.802
[Captain:] According to the duty roster, Whitney, it is your turn.

00:30:39.802 --> 00:30:42.068
No one's trying to take advantage of you.

00:30:42.069 --> 00:30:44.069
[Hal:] He's always leaning on me.

00:30:44.069 --> 00:30:46.169
The whole outfit's down on me.

00:30:46.169 --> 00:30:48.069
I want out. I want to transfer.

00:30:48.069 --> 00:30:49.635
[Captain:] I won't recommend you for a transfer.

00:30:49.636 --> 00:30:50.836
That's out of the question.

00:30:50.836 --> 00:30:55.436
[Hal:] If you won't do it, I'll see somebody in the IG office who will. I can't get a fair shake around here.

00:30:55.436 --> 00:30:58.502
[Officer:] It's not within the jurisdiction of this office to give you a transfer.

00:30:58.502 --> 00:31:00.168
That's up to your company commander.

00:31:00.169 --> 00:31:03.735
[Hal:] That's why I came to see the IG, sir. I'm hoping you can help me out of this.

00:31:03.736 --> 00:31:08.902
[Officer:] Private Whitney, according to our investigation, you have not proved to be the most cooperative man in your outfit.

00:31:08.902 --> 00:31:12.002
You borrow money, you borrow equipment without returning it.

00:31:12.002 --> 00:31:14.468
[Hal:] I don't get a chance, sir.

00:31:14.469 --> 00:31:16.835
[Officer:] Month after month the reports have come down.

00:31:16.836 --> 00:31:19.669
You have got to follow certain routines.

00:31:19.669 --> 00:31:24.569
[Hal:] I try, sir. I try, but I can't, if they're persecuting me, I can't...

00:31:24.569 --> 00:31:27.202
you can't drive a man and expect him to do his best job, sir.

00:31:27.202 --> 00:31:30.268
[Chief:] OK, buddy. You're just like anyone else.

00:31:30.269 --> 00:31:33.602
You're on pots and pans and that's it. So, let's go.

00:31:33.602 --> 00:31:35.935
[Hal:] I've got a cut on my finger, I don't want to get it infected.

00:31:35.936 --> 00:31:38.336
[Chief:] Well, we've got a special antiseptic detergent.

00:31:38.336 --> 00:31:42.636
It's great for infections. Move out!

00:31:42.636 --> 00:31:46.202
[Hal:] Everybody's a big wheel. Everybody takes a crack at you.

00:31:46.202 --> 00:31:48.168
What a lousy deal!

00:31:48.169 --> 00:31:50.802
[Soldier:] Hey, Whitney, you know this isn't exactly a hobby with me.

00:31:50.802 --> 00:31:52.735
We're in on this detail together, remember?

00:31:52.736 --> 00:31:58.336
[Chief:] OK, OK. Come on, cut the chatter! Let's get these pots and pans clean.

00:31:58.340 --> 00:32:00.336
[Hal:] I've had it! You hear?

00:32:00.340 --> 00:32:02.740
I'm getting out of here and I'm not coming back!

00:32:02.740 --> 00:32:05.460
If they want me, they're gonna have to come and take me in a box!

00:32:16.860 --> 00:32:20.220
[Narrator:] Later, Whitney is rounded up by the MPs in a local bar.

00:32:20.236 --> 00:32:24.636
The roaring lion hasn't been reduced to a lamb completely,

00:32:24.636 --> 00:32:31.602
but there's no denying that his big, bold gesture of defiance has turned out to be only a flurry of futility.

00:32:31.602 --> 00:32:34.302
Whitney loses again.

00:32:34.302 --> 00:32:43.235
[ Music ]

00:32:43.236 --> 00:32:47.002
[Captain:] I've just about had it with you, Whitney. I gave you an Article 15 before.

00:32:47.002 --> 00:32:50.168
Now I'm putting you up for a summary court-martial.

00:32:50.169 --> 00:32:53.402
And, in the meantime, I'm restricting you to your barracks.

00:32:53.402 --> 00:32:54.960
[Hal:] But, sir, you... [Captain:] That'll be all!

00:32:54.960 --> 00:32:59.820
[ Music ]

00:32:59.920 --> 00:33:03.102
[Narrator:] It is true that Hal Whitney is now boxed into a corner.

00:33:03.102 --> 00:33:04.535
A man in serious trouble.

00:33:04.536 --> 00:33:09.436
It is also true that with his outbursts, his inability to get along with others,

00:33:09.436 --> 00:33:16.836
and his overreactions to frustration, he exhibits clues in common with Phil Owens, Chris Warren, and Laura Benson.

00:33:16.836 --> 00:33:22.302
But, unlike that trio, doesn't Hal Whitney have the strength of his own aggressive nature?

00:33:22.302 --> 00:33:25.935
The energy of his own brash personality to keep him going?

00:33:25.936 --> 00:33:31.636
Wouldn't he, a man who has never taken anything laying down, continue to look for some way

00:33:31.636 --> 00:33:34.002
to outwit the world around him?

00:33:34.002 --> 00:33:46.060
[ Music ]

00:33:46.060 --> 00:33:50.300
Nobody's going to court-martial Hal Whitney. Hell, no.

00:33:50.302 --> 00:33:54.435
He's thought of a way to show them all he's not kidding when he says he's had it.

00:33:54.436 --> 00:34:06.302
[ Music ]

00:34:06.302 --> 00:34:11.668
Whether an immature bid for attention, a true suicide attempt, or perhaps a combination of both,

00:34:11.669 --> 00:34:16.035
Hal Whitney's action verifies extreme emotional disturbance.

00:34:16.036 --> 00:34:20.036
It is the act of a man in need of professional attention.

00:34:20.040 --> 00:34:29.060
[ Music ]

00:34:29.060 --> 00:34:34.636
Now meet Charles Walters, an active man whose bounce belies his 50 years.

00:34:34.636 --> 00:34:40.502
A man who welcomes any physical challenge as an opportunity to demonstrate his agility and strength.

00:34:40.502 --> 00:34:45.435
[ Music ]

00:34:45.436 --> 00:34:48.802
But this exercise is his bid for disaster.

00:34:48.802 --> 00:34:55.880
A defiant flexing of muscle with which he seeks to thumb his nose at the unhappy events of recent weeks.

00:34:58.540 --> 00:35:02.300
[Doctor:] Well, there's not much question about it, Chuck.

00:35:02.300 --> 00:35:04.900
You had a mild case of angina pectoris.

00:35:06.080 --> 00:35:10.060
[Charles:] Angina pectoris? What, do ya mean a cardiac?

00:35:10.069 --> 00:35:13.002
[Doctor:] Well, I don't think labels like that mean very much, Chuck.

00:35:13.002 --> 00:35:19.568
You see, the problem is that sometimes your heart muscle doesn't get as much blood as it should.

00:35:19.569 --> 00:35:24.069
[Charles:] I've always been in such good condition. I feel like an invalid.

00:35:24.069 --> 00:35:25.569
[Doctor:] Well, you're not an invalid, Chuck.

00:35:25.569 --> 00:35:28.435
You've got this condition and you're going to have to learn to live with it.

00:35:28.436 --> 00:35:30.136
Now, it won't be all that hard.

00:35:30.136 --> 00:35:32.969
Go and get dressed, Chuck.

00:35:32.969 --> 00:35:35.160
Go and get dressed, Chuck.

00:35:39.469 --> 00:35:46.520
[Narrator:] With a few simple words, the doctor had erased an image that Charles Walters had been a lifetime building.

00:35:46.536 --> 00:35:49.969
The image of a virile, athletic, rugged individual.

00:35:49.969 --> 00:35:51.969
Proud and self-reliant.

00:35:51.969 --> 00:35:59.235
What was left, by his own set of values, was a useless shell, something of which to be ashamed.

00:35:59.236 --> 00:36:04.202
[Charles:] Taken? [Other Soldiers:] No, have a seat.

00:36:04.202 --> 00:36:06.502
[Young Soldier:] All right, Chuck. You got it all planned?

00:36:06.502 --> 00:36:10.535
Going to get one of those high-paying executive jobs?

00:36:10.536 --> 00:36:12.636
[Charles:] No. Nobody wants an old man like me.

00:36:12.636 --> 00:36:17.336
I think I'll just sit around and watch you young fellas take over.

00:36:17.336 --> 00:36:19.769
[Young Soldier:] Young fellas.

00:36:19.769 --> 00:36:25.502
[Narrator:] When friends and fellow officers had joked lightly about his forthcoming medical retirement,

00:36:25.502 --> 00:36:31.160
he had joined right in with the banter, but already he felt a growing sense of isolation.

00:36:32.968 --> 00:36:39.760
The phasing-out process that gradually separated him from his duties and responsibilities drained him of self-respect.

00:36:39.769 --> 00:36:46.502
For him, the rapidly approaching end of a productive Army career would also be the end of his useful life.

00:36:46.520 --> 00:36:50.736
His fellow officers thought a certain amount of despondency was normal.

00:36:50.740 --> 00:36:53.169
They were confident that he would adjust.

00:36:53.169 --> 00:36:55.180
That he would snap out of it.

00:37:00.469 --> 00:37:02.900
They did not know.

00:37:02.902 --> 00:37:10.735
They could not see that Charles Walters was drifting with steadily increasing momentum toward despair.

00:37:10.736 --> 00:37:21.169
[ Music ]

00:37:21.169 --> 00:37:23.100
[Charles:] I don't know. Suddenly my life is over.

00:37:25.140 --> 00:37:27.000
[Terri:] That isn't so, dear.

00:37:27.002 --> 00:37:29.035
Why, there's a lot you can do.

00:37:29.036 --> 00:37:32.136
We're not going to starve.

00:37:32.136 --> 00:37:36.602
[Charles:] Yeah? Who wants a retired infantry officer with a bum heart?

00:37:36.602 --> 00:37:41.035
[Terri:] Well, you could do some coaching. You've always loved sports.

00:37:41.036 --> 00:37:42.936
Don said he'd get you a job at his school.

00:37:42.936 --> 00:37:47.502
All you have to do is work up a resume for it.

00:37:47.502 --> 00:37:51.068
[Charles:] Yeah. A lot of things I can do.

00:37:51.068 --> 00:37:54.960
But, let's be honest, Terri, I can't do anything!

00:37:58.600 --> 00:38:03.236
I can't even be a husband to you anymore.

00:38:03.236 --> 00:38:04.836
[Terri:] Chuck.

00:38:04.836 --> 00:38:11.336
[ Music ]

00:38:11.336 --> 00:38:15.702
[Narrator:] Chuck Walters, an invalid? He'd rather die first.

00:38:15.702 --> 00:38:19.968
[ Music ]

00:38:19.969 --> 00:38:23.369
[Weights hastily dropped clang together at the gym.]

00:38:23.369 --> 00:38:30.469
[Music]

00:38:30.469 --> 00:38:35.502
The intense flurry of exercise that precipitates Charles Walters' heart attack is unquestionably

00:38:35.502 --> 00:38:38.768
a suicidal act, whether conscious or unconscious.

00:38:38.769 --> 00:38:48.102
Luckily, the attack is not fatal and perhaps this close sideswipe with death will bring to him a new awareness of the joy of living.

00:38:48.102 --> 00:38:51.002
Perhaps now the fog of depression will lift,

00:38:51.002 --> 00:38:55.168
and he will see the brightness that is possible in his future.

00:38:55.169 --> 00:39:07.335
[ Music ]

00:39:07.336 --> 00:39:10.169
There has been no torrent of enthusiasm or joy,

00:39:10.169 --> 00:39:16.335
but in the months since his attack, his wife has seen in Charles Walters a more relaxed human being.

00:39:16.336 --> 00:39:18.369
Less frustrated by inactivity.

00:39:18.369 --> 00:39:20.402
More inclined to smile.

00:39:20.402 --> 00:39:26.302
Though still maintaining a watchful eye, she begins to feel that her vigil is no longer urgent.

00:39:26.302 --> 00:39:29.235
Her husband's desperate act is behind him.

00:39:29.236 --> 00:39:33.336
A black moment that has passed and can be forgotten.

00:39:33.336 --> 00:39:34.702
[Terri:] Are you sure you feel all right?

00:39:34.702 --> 00:39:38.135
[Charles:] Oh, I'm fine. Don't worry about me.

00:39:38.136 --> 00:39:40.502
[Terri:] Why don't you work on that resume now?

00:39:40.502 --> 00:39:42.035
It should only take you an hour or so.

00:39:42.036 --> 00:39:46.802
[Charles:] OK. I'll give it a whirl. No time like the present.

00:39:46.802 --> 00:39:51.668
[Terri:] Good. Chuck, you seem so much better. You sure you feel all right?

00:39:51.669 --> 00:39:55.069
I won't disturb you now. You go on working.

00:39:55.069 --> 00:39:57.902
I have some water on for tea.

00:39:57.902 --> 00:40:06.568
[Narrator:] But truth, unvarnished by hopeful optimism, tells a different story.

00:40:06.569 --> 00:40:10.635
The improvement that follows a suicidal attempt is frequently misleading.

00:40:10.636 --> 00:40:15.069
A mask concealing the return of depression and thoughts of suicide.

00:40:15.069 --> 00:40:20.969
Nothing has really changed for Charles Walters, who still sees himself as an invalid.

00:40:20.969 --> 00:40:24.335
Inadequate, defeated, a man without a future.

00:40:24.336 --> 00:40:28.702
His wife should be insisting that he seek professional help,

00:40:28.702 --> 00:40:33.568
but she has heard and believed that once a man attempts suicide and fails,

00:40:33.569 --> 00:40:35.802
he will never try again.

00:40:35.802 --> 00:40:43.200
She has been diverted by a myth and this myth, like so many others concerning suicide, can be fatal.

00:40:52.340 --> 00:41:08.200
[Sound of a drawer being gently opened and closed, then silence while Charles contemplates.]

00:41:28.240 --> 00:41:35.220
[The teakettle whistles, then a gunshot is heard.]

00:41:38.269 --> 00:41:41.060
[Terri, screaming:] Oh! No, Chuck!

00:41:41.060 --> 00:41:48.260
[ Sobbing ]

00:41:52.640 --> 00:41:55.769
[Narrator:] Suicide is not a remote, rare occurrence.

00:41:55.769 --> 00:41:59.802
It has in fact become one of our most serious national health problems.

00:41:59.802 --> 00:42:03.135
But most suicides are not inevitable.

00:42:03.136 --> 00:42:10.736
Prevention is possible, if the potential suicide is recognized and if we learn where myth ends and truth begins.

00:42:10.736 --> 00:42:13.902
Remember, suicide is not always sudden.

00:42:13.902 --> 00:42:17.068
It is almost always preceded by danger signs,

00:42:17.068 --> 00:42:21.040
clues indicating severe emotional disturbance and depression.

00:42:25.702 --> 00:42:31.836
Remember, too, that the potential suicide is not necessarily a meek, quiet, helpless mouse.

00:42:31.836 --> 00:42:35.302
He can appear to be an aggressive, assertive individual.

00:42:35.302 --> 00:42:39.502
It is not true that if they talk about it, they never do it.

00:42:39.502 --> 00:42:43.535
It is not true that an unsuccessful attempt is never repeated.

00:42:43.536 --> 00:42:54.402
A period of seeming improvement can be fatally deceptive, for real progress results from change of circumstance and professional help.

00:42:54.402 --> 00:42:58.735
Remember that most potential suicides can be helped.

00:42:58.736 --> 00:43:02.436
Assistance can take the simple form of friendliness, which might in some instances

00:43:02.436 --> 00:43:10.936
relieve unbearable depression and loneliness, and which we are all equipped to offer.

00:43:10.936 --> 00:43:16.036
Beyond that, we should direct the emotionally disturbed person to professional help.

00:43:16.040 --> 00:43:23.160
The chaplain, the general medical officer, the psychiatrist.

00:43:27.120 --> 00:43:34.460
These have been faces of fiction, but the tragedies they have lived in light and shadow are duplicated daily

00:43:34.469 --> 00:43:37.969
by flesh and blood people in everyday life.

00:43:37.969 --> 00:43:39.869
You may know one of them.

00:43:39.869 --> 00:43:42.135
Today or perhaps tomorrow.

00:43:42.136 --> 00:43:49.669
If so, it is possible that his or her survival could depend upon your awareness of the suicide problem,

00:43:49.669 --> 00:43:53.069
your ability to recognize a serious emotional disturbance,

00:43:53.069 --> 00:44:00.320
your interest and concern in the troubles of another human being.

00:44:00.880 --> 00:44:14.820
[Music]

